# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: string-split-argument-order -- STRING-SPLIT takes (separator string), the spec says (string delimiter)
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: builtin
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-10-20

* Symptom

Found while preparing the E03 (dialects) demo of the video series, on
clautolisp 2.3.24:

#+begin_example
$ clautolisp --bricscad -x '(print (string-split "a,b,c" ","))'
("" "")
#+end_example

The BricsCAD V26 DevRef, as recorded in autolisp-spec (Function Entry:
STRING-SPLIT), gives the syntax =(string-split string delimiter)=; the
expected result is =("a" "b" "c")=.

* Cause

=STRING-SPLIT= is registered on =builtin-vl-string-split=
(=clautolisp/autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp=), whose lambda list is
=(separator source)= -- the order of BricsCAD's =VL-STRING-SPLIT=. The call
above therefore splits ="," on any of "a,b,c"='s characters.

* To do

- Check the argument order of both =STRING-SPLIT= and =VL-STRING-SPLIT=
  against the BricsCAD DevRef (and a vendor probe: =probes/sources/=) --
  the comment in =builtin-vl-string-split= calls =string-split= an "ad-hoc
  alias", which the spec contradicts.
- Give =STRING-SPLIT= its own entry point with the documented order; add a
  failing test first.
- Whether a multi-character delimiter splits on the string or on each of
  its characters is not documented: record it as SPEC-UNCERTAIN until probed.
